--- Issue Found in Initial Packet Decryption ---

The bug is in src/quic/crypto_quic.zig line 440-445:

CURRENT CODE (WRONG):
```zig
// RFC 9001: sample starts 4 bytes after the start of packet number field
// Since ciphertext_and_tag starts after PN, offset is always 4
const offset = 4;
if (offset + 16 > ciphertext_and_tag.len) return error.AuthenticationFailed;
@memcpy(sample16[0..], ciphertext_and_tag[offset .. offset + 16]);
```

THE PROBLEM:
The comment says "sample starts 4 bytes after the start of packet number field"
but the code is taking the sample from offset 4 in the ciphertext, which starts AFTER the PN field.

According to RFC 9001 Section 5.4.2:
"The sample of ciphertext is taken starting from an offset of 4 bytes after the start
of the Packet Number field."

So if the PN field is N bytes long, the sample should start at:
- Masked PN bytes: [0..N)
- Ciphertext starts at position N
- Sample starts at position 4 (which is 4-N bytes into the ciphertext if N < 4)

CORRECT CODE:
```zig
// RFC 9001: sample starts 4 bytes after the start of packet number field
// If PN is N bytes, sample starts at (4-N) bytes into ciphertext
const sample_offset = if (pn_len >= 4) 0 else (4 - pn_len);
if (sample_offset + 16 > ciphertext_and_tag.len) return error.AuthenticationFailed;
@memcpy(sample16[0..], ciphertext_and_tag[sample_offset .. sample_offset + 16]);
```

This explains why the Zig-to-Zig communication works (they both have the same bug)
but external clients fail (they follow the RFC correctly).